{
  "statement": "This debate is not about whether we trust AI. It is about who holds the stop button when software runs the power grid, the water supply, or the financial rails that a hundred million people depend on every day. In every other mission-critical sector, the state already holds that final stop power. The Federal Aviation Administration grounded the entire Boeing 737 MAX fleet in March 2019 after two crashes killed 346 people, and no one argued Boeing should decide for itself when to stop flying. Nuclear command authority has never been delegated to the plant operator. Grid reliability standards under federal law are mandatory, not suggested. AI placed inside those same systems belongs to that category by function, not by novelty. The question opponents must answer is simple: why should an AI operator be the one exception — the sole judge of when its own system stops?\n\nThe trigger conditions for a mandated kill-switch should be three: demonstrated capability, operational autonomy, and control over critical infrastructure. Where those three converge, shutdown authority cannot be delegated to the firm being overseen. That delegation is what public administration scholars call capture by design — the referee is employed by the team. Oversight that cannot halt a system at the speed the system fails is not oversight. A grid protection event unfolds in seconds, sometimes milliseconds. No human approval chain meets that window unless shutdown authority is pre-authorized, tested, and partly automated before deployment. The record of an absent kill-switch causing harm is thin so far — that is honest. But the physics of response time is not speculation, and waiting for the first cascade failure to justify the switch means the proof arrives with the body count.\n\nThe strongest argument against this position deserves a fair hearing. Concentrating shutdown power in the state creates its own dangers: a less liberal government could use the same override authority to silence critics or punish rivals, and regulators who sit close enough to understand frontier systems tend to adopt the industry's worldview. Both risks are real. The answer is not to leave the power with the operator — it is to design the institution so abuse is expensive. Dual authorization for any shutdown order. Judicial review after the fact. Sunset clauses that force legislatures to renew the mandate. Published trigger criteria that change only on measurable, pre-committed evidence, so no official can quietly loosen a threshold for a favored firm. Third-party harm complaint channels that bypass the operator entirely. These are the same tools that keep financial and energy regulators honest, imperfectly but demonstrably.\n\nWhat an ordinary citizen should watch for is whether any proposal has teeth in these three places: a named public authority with legal power to halt a system, trigger conditions written into law rather than negotiated case-by-case with the firms, and a complaint channel that does not run through the company being watched. Voluntary safety frameworks already exist at major AI labs, tiered by capability — the only live question is whether those tiers become binding floors for infrastructure or remain press releases. A kill-switch that exists only in a company's goodwill is not a safeguard. It is a promise. And a promise is exactly what the last century of industrial regulation — from the 2008 financial collapse to the 737 MAX — taught us not to build critical systems on.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔌", "tooltip": "The kill-switch as the physical plug the public must be able to pull, not a promise that someone else will pull it."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Dual authorization and judicial review as the counterweights that keep state shutdown power from becoming state abuse."},
    {"symbol": "✈️", "tooltip": "The 737 MAX grounding as the standing precedent: regulators, not manufacturers, decide when a dangerous system stops."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "In every other mission-critical sector, the state already holds final stop power, and AI placed inside those systems belongs to that category by function.", "targets": [], "relationship": "core thesis"},
    {"claim": "The FAA grounded the Boeing 737 MAX fleet in March 2019 after two crashes killed 346 people, and no one argued Boeing should decide for itself when to stop flying.", "targets": [], "relationship": "precedent evidence"},
    {"claim": "The trigger conditions for a mandated kill-switch should be three: demonstrated capability, operational autonomy, and control over critical infrastructure.", "targets": [], "relationship": "policy specification"},
    {"claim": "A grid protection event unfolds in seconds, sometimes milliseconds, and no human approval chain meets that window unless shutdown authority is pre-authorized before deployment.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting premise"},
    {"claim": "The record of an absent kill-switch causing harm is thin so far — that is honest — but the physics of response time is not speculation.", "targets": [], "relationship": "pre-emptive concession"},
    {"claim": "Concentrating shutdown power in the state creates its own dangers, and the answer is dual authorization, judicial review, sunset clauses, and published trigger criteria.", "targets": [], "relationship": "objection steelman and rebuttal"},
    {"claim": "A kill-switch that exists only in a company's goodwill is not a safeguard; the 2008 financial collapse and the 737 MAX taught us not to build critical systems on promises.", "targets": [], "relationship": "closing consequence"}
  ]
