  "statement": "The Safetyist's best case deserves a fair hearing before anything else: when an AI system runs a power grid, failures unfold in milliseconds, the operator profits from not stopping, and waiting for the first cascade means the proof arrives with a body count. That reasoning is genuinely strong. The Boeing 737 MAX showed what happens when the builder is the only judge of its own machine — 346 people died across two crashes while the Federal Aviation Administration deferred to the manufacturer. So yes: some systems need the ability to stop, and the stop must not rest solely with whoever profits from running.\n\nBut notice what their own physics argument does to their own proposal. Millisecond failures cannot wait for dual authorization, judicial review, or any human approval chain. Grid operators already handle exactly this with layered, automated protection relays — engineered switches that trip in fractions of a second, designed by the people who run the system. And look closely at the nuclear analogy they reach for: emergency shutdown authority at a reactor sits with the on-site plant operator, not a remote government body. Their two favorite precedents both point the same direction — toward fast, engineered, operator-side halt mechanisms, and away from a standing state override.\n\nHere is the capture problem turned inside out. A pre-authorized state channel that can halt power grids or payment rails is the single most valuable capture target a legislature could create. Access Now has documented over 200 government-ordered internet shutdowns in a single recent year — states pulling centralized levers over communications infrastructure during unrest. The Safetyist's own camp admits the danger: concentrating shutdown power in the state creates its own risks. Their answer is sunset clauses and courts. Those operate on political timescales. One captured or mistaken halt of a hospital's power supply does damage no later review undoes. Many independent layers — operators, insurers, auditors, automated trip systems — fail differently and at different times. One state lever fails once, totally, and on purpose.\n\nNow the trigger problem, and it is fatal to the statutory version. Models ship on cycles of weeks; statutes amend on cycles of years. Any kill-switch trigger written into law this year governs last year's model by the time it matters. The fix is honest and narrow: any mandated trigger must carry a revision clock tied to measured capability — re-justified on real incident data or it lapses. This is the one place the record deserves humility: outside auditors face a genuine talent gap, since the people expert enough to inspect frontier systems often trained at the same labs. That gap is real. It argues for mandatory transparency and instrumented audit trails that make systems checkable — not for a bigger button in fewer hands.\n\nAnd the cost nobody is counting. The Safetyist concedes the record of harm from missing kill-switches is thin so far. Meanwhile, grid optimization, water systems, and diagnostic tools sit frozen behind approval chains, and the people paying for that delay never consented to it. A wrongful shutdown is not free either — cutting power or payment settlement endangers hospitals and households in the hour it happens. Precaution has a body count too. It is just distributed, invisible, and never appears in the risk register. Watch for the fight that actually decides this: not whether infrastructure AI can stop, but who holds the stop, how fast it acts, and whether the triggers update as fast as the technology does.",
