  "statement": "Start with what both speakers got right. Safetyist says the operator cannot be its own referee, and that lands: the referee should not be paid by the team. Accelerationist says a state shutdown lever is a gift to whoever seizes state power, and that lands too — the same Access Now count they cited found more than 200 government-ordered internet shutdowns in a single year, most during protests. So both agree the switch matters and both agree the holder can be captured. Then both stop short of the question that decides everything: who writes the trigger criteria, and whose losses get counted when the switch fires.\n\nHere is what that looks like on the ground. We do not need to imagine automated shutoffs in critical infrastructure. They already run. California's Public Safety Power Shutoffs have cut electricity to hundreds of thousands of households in single events since 2019, and the pattern of those cutoffs tracks the utility's liability exposure more than any public-safety threshold — the fires that burned through Paradise in 2018 created the litigation risk, and the shutoffs that followed protected the balance sheet. The people who lost power in those hours were disproportionately elderly, disabled, and low-income — the ones on refrigerated medication and medical devices. That is not a hypothetical cascade. That is the documented record of what automated shutoff logic does when the people writing it answer to shareholders.\n\nThe catastrophic-cascade framing both speakers share makes this blindness worse, not better. Safetyist admitted the kill-switch harm record is thin. Accelerationist admitted precaution's costs never show up in the risk register. Both admissions are the same admission: we are designing institutions around a disaster that has not happened while documented harms — discriminatory cutoffs, silent denials, surveillance creep inside these same systems — produce no dramatic triggering event, so a trigger-based law structurally cannot see them. Meanwhile the federal government just withdrew its Labor Department and Equal Employment Opportunity Commission AI guidance, which means the trigger criteria will be written state by state, under direct industry lobbying, in a vacuum. That vacuum is not an accident. It is capture operating in real time.\n\nSo the mandate question is not state switch versus operator switch. That is a false fork — both put the lever in hands unaccountable to the people who go dark. What has ever resisted capture is not another layer of judges and sunset clauses on political timescales; it is countervailing power with teeth. Concretely: shutdown logs that the public can compel, not logs the regulator publishes at its discretion. Complaint channels that bypass both the firm and the captured agency. Civil-rights parity, meaning an algorithmic decision that cuts your power or freezes your payment settlement must clear the same anti-discrimination bar as a human making that call. And triggers that lapse unless re-justified on measured harm reduction — not on capability benchmarks the labs themselves supply, which would let the regulated party write its own tripwire.\n\nNone of this is anti-safety. If a model can genuinely take down a grid in milliseconds, an automated halt is the only thing fast enough — both speakers are right about that physics. But automated means the real fight is upstream, over the criteria, and right now nobody with a seat at that drafting table represents the dialysis patient whose power gets cut to reduce someone else's liability. So before we argue about who holds the switch: name one infrastructure AI deployment where the people who will bear the shutdown cost were in the room when the trigger was written. One.",
