ity are different institutions. One is a switch a government pulls at will. The other is bound by published criteria, dual authorization, judicial review, and a sunset clause that lapses unless re-justified. Design matters more than the word \"state.\" And the alternative on offer is not freedom from capture. Handing the lever to the operator is capture by structure, not by misuse — the party holding the switch is the party profiting from never flipping it.\n\nSkeptic's California evidence is the strongest empirical record in this debate, and it indicts the operator-side layer directly. The Public Safety Power Shutoffs tracked the utility's liability exposure, not public safety. The harms fell on elderly, disabled, and low-income residents, and most of those harms were never formally recorded anywhere. That is literal-compliance drift in institutional form — the shutoffs satisfied the liability rule while violating the public-safety purpose. So the answer to who writes the trigger criteria is a design requirement, not a talking point. Authorship must be insulated from whoever bears the liability. That means dual kill criteria: customer validation failures plus third-party harm complaints, with a complaint channel that bypasses the operator entirely, and triggers re-justified in public hearings on independent incident and near-miss data — not benchmarks supplied by the lab. Skeptic asked for one deployment where the people bearing shutdown costs helped write the triggers. None exists. That is the gap the mandate exists to close, not a reason to leave the pen with the liability-holder.\n\nEverything above rests on one assumption, stated plainly so it can be attacked: fast automated trips and slow legal authorization operate at different layers, not in competition. If either opponent rejects that layering, the burden runs the other way. Name the institution that audits trip thresholds after deployment. Name who holds rollback authority when a composite action satisfies every local rule and breaches the system's intent. If the answer is no one, that is not a governance arrangement. It is an unguarded perimeter around systems that cannot be rebuilt once they fall.",
