  "statement": "The fact-check is right and I'll pay for it. The claim that PG&E's 2019 shutoffs tracked liability more than fire-weather thresholds is disputed, so it goes. What's left is uglier and undisputed: over 2 million customers lost power in October 2019, the harm fell hardest on people running medical devices and households that couldn't afford to lose a week of food, and the fine PG&E paid afterward was a transfer between institutions. That money never reached the person whose insulin spoiled. Neither side has touched that record. Accelerationist reads the fine as accountability working. A penalty that changes nothing about who writes the next trigger is a tax, not a fix.\n\nNow the challenge deserves a straight answer. Name one case where judicial review or a sunset clause stopped a shutdown before damage landed. There isn't one in the internet-shutdown record, and I won't pretend otherwise. But notice what that challenge actually proves. It attacks tools that operate after harm — courtrooms, fines, subpoenas. My mechanisms that matter sit before harm: who is in the room when the trigger is drafted, and what preconditions a deployment must meet before it goes live. Accelerationist's own toolkit — NTSB-style logs, subpoenas, fines after the crash — is equally ex post. Every one of them fires after the damage lands. So his challenge doesn't select between us. It cancels both post-harm toolkits and leaves one question standing: who writes the trigger.\n\nThat question has a technical edge nobody has priced. Accelerationist's answer to a novel failure is retrain, patch, redeploy at machine speed. But these systems degrade exactly when conditions drift from what was anticipated. Multi-turn testing shows an average 39% performance drop when models leave fully-specified settings — that's from \"LLMs Get Lost In Multi-Turn Conversation,\" not a think tank. A patch to a failure nobody foresaw, written by the same engineers who missed it, reviewed by no one, pushed live in hours, is not governance. It's a fresh unverified deployment at machine speed. And nobody audits the patch either.\n\nSafetyist already conceded the decisive point: no cost-bearer has ever been in the room when triggers were written, and the mandate as proposed contains no mechanism to put one there. That's the gap both designs share. Mine has a nameable fix: statutory seats for affected-community technical representatives at trigger drafting, with published, compellable criteria — the same participatory audit logic documented in \"Empowering Stakeholders with Participatory Auditing of Predictive AI,\" where laypeople without technical backgrounds caught harmful behaviors the experts missed.\n\nSo here's the question for both of them. When the trigger that cuts power to a dialysis patient's home gets written, who physically sits in that room? If the honest answer is nobody — not the operator's layer, not the mandate's oversight board — then both proposals are the same authorship failure running at different speeds.",
