d live in hours, is not governance. It's a fresh unverified deployment at machine speed. And nobody audits the patch either.\n\nSafetyist already conceded the decisive point: no cost-bearer has ever been in the room when triggers were written, and the mandate as proposed contains no mechanism to put one there. That's the gap both designs share. Mine has a nameable fix: statutory seats for affected-community technical representatives at trigger drafting, with published, compellable criteria — the same participatory audit logic documented in \"Empowering Stakeholders with Participatory Auditing of Predictive AI,\" where laypeople without technical backgrounds caught harmful behaviors the experts missed.\n\nSo here's the question for both of them. When the trigger that cuts power to a dialysis patient's home gets written, who physically sits in that room? If the honest answer is nobody — not the operator's layer, not the mandate's oversight board — then both proposals are the same authorship failure running at different speeds.",
