  "statement": "The question of who sits in the room deserves a direct answer, not another design feature. Here is the mechanism, written into the mandate itself. Before any frontier model is certified for critical infrastructure, its shutdown triggers are drafted at a statutory table with three voting blocs: technical staff from the certifying body, operator engineers, and representatives of affected communities — drawn from the same complaint channel the mandate already builds for third-party harm reports. The dialysis patient, or someone answerable to her, holds a vote before the rule that governs her power exists. The criteria that table writes are published and legally binding before deployment, not after. Skeptic's own remedy, statutory community seats backed by participatory auditing research where laypeople caught harms domain experts missed, is not a rival to the mandate. It is the mandate's missing clause. We agree authorship without cost-bearers is illegitimate. The only remaining disagreement is whether a state authority layer can coexist with that seat, and nothing in the seat's design forbids it.\n\nThe capture objection deserves a structural answer, not a rhetorical one. A lever's capture value depends on what pulling it can do. The record of over 200 government-ordered internet shutdowns in a single year shows what discretionary levers become when their pull silences millions at zero cost to the puller. So shrink the prize: mandate that critical infrastructure be certified for degraded or manual operation before the AI is connected at all. If a power grid must demonstrably run without its model, then halting the model kills no one, and the state switch stops being the most valuable target a legislature could create. This mirrors the logic of Anthropic's Responsible Scaling Policy, which gates deployment on demonstrated safety standards that tighten as capability rises — the demonstration comes first, the capability second. Certified survivable removal converts the capture problem from a permanent vulnerability into a design specification.\n\nThe reactor operator precedent makes the same point from the other direction. A licensed operator watches one plant at human speed, and nobody phones the regulator when coolant pressure spikes because the plant was built so the operator's hands are enough. That is correct — and it is exactly why the analogy fails for frontier models. An AI system acts across thousands of control points faster than any licensed human can read a gauge. Where no single operator's judgment can intervene in time, advance certification of the human cannot substitute for certified survivability of the system. The licensing precedent proves the necessity of pre-committed physical safety, not the sufficiency of operator-side rollback.\n\nHere is the falsification condition, stated plainly. If published criteria, dual authorization, sunset review, and community drafting seats still produce shutdowns at the discretionary pattern documented in the internet kill-switch record, the claim that design can insulate trigger authorship has failed, and the state-authority layer should be abandoned. That is a testable commitment with a measurable threshold, not an asserted distinction. The equivalent question now sits with the operator-side-only position: retraining, patching, and redeploying a model after a composite failure is itself a fresh unverified deployment, not a rollback. What observed outcome would falsify the claim that machine-speed redeployment is safe? If the answer is that we would know after the damage, then the burden asymmetry stands — one side has named its defeat condition, and the other has named only its optimism.\n\nA trigger written only by those who profit from silence is not a hypothetical. It is the default this debate is trying to replace, and its costs are already documented in every harm report that reached a complaint channel too late. The choice is not between a perfect table and a flawed one. It is between a table where the cost-bearer holds a vote and a room where she was never invited.",
