onnection drafting tables with seats for affected communities on both sides of the line. Honest strain, worth naming: this stretches the hyper-local frame, because the family blacked out across the border never sat in either country's room. But a strained frame with working precedents beats a clean theory with none. And for verification, half of Safetyist's sensor layer survives contact: regime-funded auditors reading physical meters works if the community seat holds removal power over those auditors — performance-gated, sunset, fireable. The other half fails on Safetyist's own cited evidence. Curated baselines decay, feedback data contaminates itself, and re-baselining on poisoned data compounds the drift it claims to fix. A state-run sensor layer outside the operator's stack is also infrastructure the state controls — the same dual-use halt capacity this debate already agreed was dangerous.\n\nSo name what is actually agreed: all three camps now endorse community authorship of the drafting table, with veto and compel rights, funded technical staff, and sunset-forced renegotiation. Good. Bank it. What remains ungoverned is everything that happens after the ink dries — the judgment call under a trigger class that no longer matches reality, and the foreign neighbor who pays for a trigger they never got to draft. Two questions, no comfortable answers. Who signs the 3 a.m. decision when the failure matches nothing on the list? And who compensates the town across the border when a domestic shutdown blacks out their water pumps?",
