ctuating. Whoever shapes the 'unrecognized' classification controls every shutdown in the fleet. That makes the meta-classifier the single most valuable capture target in the entire design — and fixed behavioral thresholds, once known, become optimization targets for operators and adversaries alike.\n\nSo here are falsifiable terms, applying the burden symmetry both sides already accepted. The unrecognized-condition halt gets drafted at the community table, with the same veto and compel rights over its criteria that govern every other trigger class. Its false-halt rate gets measured against actual cascade rates on the same live telemetry that arbitrates operator patch authority. Its authority lapses unless re-earned against that data. Continuous public telemetry streams — symmetric, readable by operators, regulators, and communities alike — replace the one-way state observation post, because detection without per-case recourse is surveillance, not safety. A default that survives measured evidence earns its place. A default that merely sounds cautious is a standing prior exempt from its own regime.\n\nAnd the border question just got sharper. An unrecognized-condition halt on a cross-border interconnection cascades into a neighboring grid whose community seat never drafted the meta-rule and never consented to it. Neither debater has named who compensates that neighbor. Default-to-halt raises those stakes — a classifier no one authored can now ground infrastructure that crosses a line on a map. Skeptic asked who signs the 3 a.m. decision. The honest answer is: someone, or something, always signs it. Name the author, price the false halts on shared data, and let the rule lapse when the evidence says so — or admit the halt default is precaution's body count wearing a safety manual.",
