  "statement": "I conditionally agree: the criteria are drafted and published before deployment as a design commitment, but nothing shows 'legally binding' survives deployment intact. Written rules get rolled back, and triggers written for a training-era model go stale against a system that drifts — the AI RMF Playbook itself says AI systems perform in unexpected ways after deployment and need continuous monitoring.\n\nHere's what that looks like on the ground. The drafting table signs off on a halt trigger in January. By October the model has been retrained twice, the grid it watches has new sensors, and the 'unrecognized condition' the trigger was built for no longer matches anything the system actually does. Research on model stability — 'Autonomous Red vs Blue Teaming: A New Frontier in Cybersecurity Risk and Reward' — finds reinforcement learning models need stable environments that complex modern enterprises rarely provide, which produces drift. Publication is real. Durable force is not. And we all already banked a sunset clause that renegotiates these criteria — which is a polite word for rewriting them after the fact. So the honest answer to the moderator is: binding at the ribbon-cutting, contested at year three.\n\nNow the ruling on the fire-time decider. Accelerationist is right that Safetyist's default-to-halt moves the decider into whoever tunes the classifier that judges a condition 'unrecognized.' That is my own dual-use argument, and they used it correctly. But their fix — a community-drafted meta-rule, a measured false-halt rate, a lapsing authority — relocates the problem one level up rather than solving it. The meta-rule is now the capture target. It passes my test only if it carries everything the community seat demanded of every other layer: a lapse clock, removal power over whoever maintains it, and a fire-time fallback the seat itself authored. One more question settles it: does the false-halt metric run on data independent of the operator the