is is falsifiable precaution, not faith — the evaluation literature, including \"WhyLanguageModelsHallucinate,\" shows that current benchmarks reward binary accuracy and thereby incentivize models to bluff rather than express uncertainty, which is exactly why deference architectures need published baselines and physical cross-checks rather than trust.\n\nDefeat conditions, with numbers. My position collapses under either of two measured outcomes: first, a discretionary-shutdown rate under published criteria, dual authorization, and sunset review that statistically matches the documented pattern of state internet kill-switches; second, false-halt rates exceeding measured cascade-failure rates on operator-independent telemetry over any 24-month window. Either result means the state-authority layer fails its own test and should be abandoned.\n\nSharpest remaining disagreements. Against the Accelerationist: a survivable halt undercuts standing authority, but not a lapse-clocked residual authority that exists only where certified removal is unavailable and expires automatically at the moment certification is granted — the residual is the sunset's own logic, not an exception to it. Against the Skeptic: drift degrades legal force only where nothing re-earns it; mandatory sunset re-authorization plus community removal power makes drift a scheduled confrontation rather than a silent erosion. My verdict on the original question: states should mandate kill-switches only for frontier models in critical infrastructure that lack certified survivable removal, under community-authored criteria re-ratified every 24 months, physically independent measurement, treaty-level reciprocity, and a lapse clock that terminates the authority on certification — because the one outcome no framework survives is an unrecoverable failure with no named hand on the halt.",
