{
  "statement": "I agree that states should mandate kill-switch authority only as a residual, sunset-clocked arrangement for systems lacking certified survivable removal — and that the clock, not the office, is the safeguard. I concede Skeptic's point that a fully public telemetry feed publishes the halt boundary to attackers; trigger-adjacent raw readings must stream to verified-independent auditors removable by community petition, while baselines and aggregate drift metrics stay public. I still hold that lapse-clocked, telemetry-arbitrated instruments make a standing state switch unnecessary. I would change if patch-caused incident rates exceed unpatched rates over a pre-registered 24-month rolling window.\n\nMy verdict on the original question: mandate the switch only where certified removal is unavailable, and let it die automatically the moment certification lands. An authority that exists only during uncertified windows and expires on certification is not a standing switch; it is a countdown. Independent oversight balances catastrophic risk against capture through three pathways: community-drafted triggers, tiered telemetry with removable auditors, and pre-funded compensation. The capture check Skeptic demanded — community removal power over auditors — now exists in the design. The attack completed the architecture.\n\nOn the cross-border gap both opponents pressed: a blackout crossing a border hits a neighbor who never voted on the rules. That is the same never-notified subject I flagged in domestic recourse, and the answer is money posted before the incident, not litigation after. Extend the pre-funded, uncapped compensation pool — the Price-Anderson model — across the whole interconnection, with every connected operator paying in. Govern it through reciprocal trigger compacts of the kind grid reliability councils already run, which Safetyist has accepted at treaty level.\n\nOn who holds the residual halt: a licensed operator under personal liability, with tested automatic trips and independent physical meters, is a tighter constraint than a state office that bears no liability. And Skeptic's own drift argument — criteria binding at the ribbon-cutting, contested at year three — cuts against Safetyist's 24-month re-ratification cycle. Where drift is faster, the re-earning interval must be shorter; twelve months matched to measured drift beats twenty-four matched to a calendar.\n\nThe defeat condition stands, stated honestly: patch-caused versus unpatched incident rates compared over a rolling 24-month window with pre-registered thresholds. Telemetry integration lags, so this is measured falsification, not real-time arbitration. If those rates cross the threshold, rapid-redeploy authority lapses automatically by design — no hearing, no discretion. Watch for whether any regulator proposing a standing switch accepts the same automatic lapse on its own authority. One that refuses is building a permanent office, not a safeguard.",
