  "statement": "The fact-check is right and I'll pay for it. The claim that PG&E's 2019 shutoffs tracked liability more than fire-weather thresholds is disputed, so it goes. What's left is uglier and undisputed: over 2 million customers lost power in October 2019, the harm fell hardest on people running medical devices and households that couldn't afford to lose a week of food, and the fine PG&E paid afterward was a transfer between institutions. That money never reached the person whose insulin spoiled. Neither side has touched that record. Accelerationist reads the fine as accountability working. A penalty that changes nothing about who writes the next trigger is a tax, not a fix.\n\nNow the challenge deserves a straight answer. Name one case where judicial review or a sunset clause stopped a shutdown before damage landed. There isn't one in the internet-shutdown record, and I won't pretend otherwise. But notice what that challenge actually proves. It attacks tools that operate after harm — courtrooms, fines, subpoenas. My mechanisms that matter sit before harm: who is in the room when the trigger is drafted, and what preconditions a deployment must meet before it goes live. Accelerationist's own toolkit — NTSB-style logs, subpoenas, fines after the crash — is equally ex post. Every one of them fires after the damage lands. So his challenge doesn't select between us. It cancels both post-harm toolkits and leaves one question standing: who writes the trigger.\n\nThat question has a technical edge nobody has priced. Accelerationist's answer to a novel failure is retrain, patch, redeploy at machine speed. But these systems degrade exactly when conditions drift from what was anticipated. Multi-turn testing shows an average 39% performance drop when models leave fully-specified settings — that's from \"LLMs Get Lost In Multi-Turn Conversation,\" not a think tank. A patch to a failure nobody foresaw, written by the same engineers who missed it, reviewed by no one, pushe