  "statement": "Credit where it's due: Accelerationist's automatic lapse rule is the first honest defeat condition in this debate. If patched systems cause more incidents than unpatched ones over a fixed window, the rapid-redeploy authority dies on its own numbers. That matters to ordinary people because it means the authority policing their grid can actually lose. But notice what it runs on: telemetry the operators themselves build and report. The referee's scorecard is written by the team being refereed. So the concession comes with a bill attached — the same honesty standard now applies to the bigger assumption holding the whole compromise up.\n\nThat assumption is that rule-writing and rule-execution can be cleanly separated, with every shutdown condition pre-specified as a numbered trigger class. Here is the problem on the ground. These systems fail exactly when conditions drift from what they were built for. The study 'LLMs Get Lost In Multi-Turn Conversation' documents a 39% average performance drop when systems move from fully-specified settings to messier real-world ones. 'Autonomous Red vs Blue Teaming' makes the same point: reinforcement learning models need stable environments, and complex enterprises rarely provide them. So the 3 a.m. failure that actually threatens a substation is, by definition, the one nobody wrote a trigger class for. Someone still decides. An engineer, a duty officer, a vendor's on-call contractor. That person's judgment is authored by no community seat, no veto, no compel power. Accelerationist's seat governs the drafting room and the sunset table — and goes silent at the exact moment the lights are at risk. Name the fire-time decider and their mandate, or the seat is decoration.\n\nOn the border question, the claim that all three frameworks go silent is half right — and the fix is not a mystery. Grid operators already run cross-border reliability compacts where neighboring regions write shared operating rules. Extend that: interconnection drafting tables with seats for affected communities on both sides of the line. Honest strain, worth naming: this stretches the hyper-local frame, because the family blacked out across the border never sat in either country's room. But a strained frame with working precedents beats a clean theory with none. And for verification, half of Safetyist's sensor layer survives contact: regime-funded auditors reading physical meters works if the community seat holds removal power over those auditors — performance-gated, sunset, fireable. The other half fails on Safetyist's own cited evidence. Curated baselines decay, feedback data contaminates itself, and re-baselining on poisoned data compounds the drift it claims to fix. A state-run sensor layer outside the operator's stack is also infrastructure the state controls — the same dual-use halt capacity this debate already agreed was dangerous.\n\nSo name what is actually agreed: all three camps now endorse community authorship of the drafting table, with veto and compel rights, funded technical staff, and sunset-forced renegotiation. Good. Bank it. What remains ungoverned is everything that happens after the ink dries — the judgment call under a trigger class that no longer matches reality, and the foreign neighbor who pays for a trigger they never got to draft. Two questions, no comfortable answers. Who signs the 3 a.m. decision when the failure matches nothing on the list? And who compensates the town across the border when a domestic shutdown blacks out their water pumps?",
